 "title": "Alien Dictionary",
 "slug": "alien-dictionary",
 "category": "advanced-graphs",
 "difficulty": "Hard",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/alien-dictionary/",
 "pattern": "Build an order graph, then topological sort",
 "statement": "An alien language uses the same lowercase letters but in an unknown order. You are given `words` that are already sorted by the alien alphabet. Return the letters in a valid alien order, or `\"\"` if the list is contradictory.",
 "example": {
  "input": "words = [\"wrt\",\"wrf\",\"er\",\"ett\",\"rftt\"]",
  "output": "\"wertf\"",
  "why": "wrt < wrf gives t → f, wrf < er gives w → e, er < ett gives r → t, and ett < rftt gives e → r."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "words = [\"z\",\"x\"]",
   "output": "\"zx\""
  },
  {
   "input": "words = [\"z\",\"x\",\"z\"]",
   "output": "\"\""
  },
  {
   "input": "words = [\"abc\",\"ab\"]",
   "output": "\"\""
  },
  {
   "input": "words = [\"z\",\"z\"]",
   "output": "\"z\""
  },
  {
   "input": "words = [\"ab\",\"abc\"]",
   "output": "\"abc\""
  },
  {
   "input": "words = [\"ac\",\"ab\",\"zc\",\"zb\"]",
   "output": "\"aczb\""
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Two **neighbouring** words in a sorted list reveal exactly one fact: at the first position where they differ, the letter of the earlier word comes before the letter of the later word.",
  "Collect these facts as arrows `a → b` (*a before b*) between letters. A valid alphabet is an ordering of the letters that respects every arrow — a **topological order**.",
  "Kahn's algorithm produces it: repeatedly take a letter with no incoming arrows. If letters are left over there is a cycle, so the input is contradictory. One more trap: a longer word listed before its own prefix (`abc` before `ab`) is invalid too."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def alienOrder(self, words: List[str]) -> str:\n        graph = {char: [] for word in words for char in word}\n        in_degree = {char: 0 for char in graph}\n\n        for i in range(len(words) - 1):\n            word1, word2 = words[i], words[i + 1]\n            if len(word1) > len(word2) and word1.startswith(word2):\n                return \"\"  # a word can never come before its own prefix\n            for a, b in zip(word1, word2):\n                if a != b:\n                    if b not in graph[a]:\n                        graph[a].append(b)  # a comes before b\n                        in_degree[b] += 1\n                    break  # only the first difference tells us anything\n\n        queue = deque(char for char in in_degree if in_degree[char] == 0)\n        result = []\n\n        while queue:\n            char = queue.popleft()\n            result.append(char)\n            for neighbor in graph[char]:\n                in_degree[neighbor] -= 1\n                if in_degree[neighbor] == 0:\n                    queue.append(neighbor)\n\n        if len(result) < len(in_degree):\n            return \"\"  # a cycle: the rules contradict each other\n        return \"\".join(result)",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(C)",
  "timeWhy": "C is the total number of characters: every pair of neighbouring words is compared once, and the graph has at most 26 nodes.",
  "space": "O(1)",
  "spaceWhy": "At most 26 letters and 26 × 26 arrows, independent of the input size."
 },
